Abstract
We present a method to enhance heavy-ion fusion cross-sections using nested Fibonacci frequency modulation. Rather than overcoming the Coulomb barrier through brute-force energy, two ion species are driven at their individual nuclear Giant Dipole Resonances. A primary Fibonacci sequence generates combination pulses . A secondary Fibonacci sequence injects sub-harmonic half-pulses to induce cumulative nuclear deformation.  

Simulation of $^{56}\text{Fe} + ^{208}\text{Pb} \rightarrow ^{264}\text{X}_{108}$ predicts that Pulse 5 of the nested sequence achieves a +69.0 MeV margin above the deformed Coulomb barrier at 194.0 MeV. This suggests a 100-1000x increase in fusion probability compared to random pulse timing. All code and data are released open-source to enable community replication.

Introduction
Traditional heavy-ion fusion relies on $>100$ MeV/nucleon beams to classically overcome Coulomb repulsion. This approach is energy intensive and causes significant target damage. We propose "resonance conduction": using precise temporal frequency matching to parametrically thin the barrier via collective nuclear excitation.

